  (W, "A witness program is what BIP141 calls an output script that consists of a one-byte push opcode, one of OP_0 and OP_1 to OP_16, followed by a direct data push between 2 and 40 bytes. The value of the first push is the version byte, and the following byte vector is the witness program. The book explains the same in its own words: to an old client the two pushes look like an output that anyone can spend, while to a segwit-aware client the first number is the witness version and the second part is the witness program [B141] [AH]."),
  (Y, "The definition is what lets one structure carry several kinds of authorization. With the version byte 0, the length of the program tells the two forms apart: 20 bytes is the hash of a key, 32 bytes the hash of a script, and any other length fails. Versions 1 to 16 are reserved for future extensions with no restriction: taproot is version 1. The book gives the length as the one difference that lets a full node tell the two version-0 programs apart [AH] [B141]."),
  (E, "Bitcoin Core's function IsWitnessProgram applies the rule: the script must be 4 to 42 bytes long, begin with OP_0 or OP_1 to OP_16, and have a second byte equal to the length of what follows. The page's example classifies five scripts by the rule: a 20-byte program is P2WPKH, a 32-byte one P2WSH, a 19-byte version-0 program fails, a 32-byte version-1 program is taproot, and a version-16 program is a future version. The library does the same on real spends [BCS]."),
